--TEST--
A settings object is held to the one arm it selects, so `host` beside `Driver::Sqlite` is refused while compiling and so is the server arm's key set under that driver — `rule:core-api/shape-arms-are-disjoint`, `rule:core-classes/db-capabilities`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-classes/db-capabilities`: a SQLite connection is a file. Its arm declares no `host` at
// all, because there is no server to send credentials to — and `rule:core-api/shape-flattens-at-the-abi`'s
// merged list *does* declare one, since the server arm does. So this is the
// exact literal a check against the merged key set accepts and § 2's arm
// selection refuses: one key from the arm this literal did not select.
var $file = Core\Db::open({
    driver: Core\Db\Driver::Sqlite,
    path: "/srv/shop/shop.db",
    host: "db.example.test",
});

// The other direction, and the one a key-only reading of § 2 would miss: every
// key here belongs to the server arm, and the `driver` value names the arm that
// declares none of them. An arm accepts on its values as well as its keys, so
// what is reported is the field the two arms declare differently — nothing
// declares `driver` to be a discriminant, and § 2 needs nothing to.
var $crossed = Core\Db::open({
    driver: Core\Db\Driver::Sqlite,
    host: "db.example.test",
    database: "shop",
    user: "app",
    password: "hunter2",
});
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0454]: `host` is not a key of the form this object uses
%A
error[E0401]: expected `Core\Db\Driver::MySql|Core\Db\Driver::MariaDb|Core\Db\Driver::Postgres|Core\Db\Driver::SqlServer`, found `Core\Db\Driver::Sqlite`
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
